# Normalize text in the repository AND in every working tree. Not `text=auto`
# alone: that lets core.autocrlf pick the checkout ending, and Git for Windows
# defaults to autocrlf=true — a stock clone there renders every file the
# patterns below do not pin (JSON, YAML, this file) as CRLF. `upgrade` compares
# canonical working-tree bytes against the consumer's, so those files read as
# changed on every run: 2 phantom updates per upgrade, CRLF written into the
# consumer, normalized away at commit, phantom again next run. eol=lf on the
# catch-all makes every clone byte-identical on every platform.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Shell scripts are the exception: bash executes them and shellcheck lints
# them, and both want LF. Git for Windows ships core.autocrlf=true as its
# installer default, so without this line a stock clone there rewrites every
# script to CRLF and `./joharness.sh ci` answers with SC1017 "Literal carriage
# return" on every line - 520 findings and a red gate before any work starts.
# Later patterns win, so this has to stay below the rule above.
*.sh text eol=lf

# Markdown too: handover-context.sh reads docs/handover/*.md from the working
# tree, and field() exits on any line 1 not exactly `---`. CRLF checkout empties
# every field - hook prints `[?, updated ?]`, gate above stays green.
*.md text eol=lf
